WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.340
Last time, in an adult at rest, breathing harder swung carbon dioxide and pH.

2
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:08.640
It barely moved oxygen content.

3
00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:11.400
Now carry that loop up a mountain.

4
00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.540
The air there is still about 21 percent oxygen.

5
00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:18.600
So why is the climber breathing hard?

6
00:00:22.033 --> 00:00:24.393
What altitude takes is not the fraction.

7
00:00:25.893 --> 00:00:30.673
Barometric pressure falls, and the pressure of oxygen in every breath falls with it.

8
00:00:31.733 --> 00:00:32.833
The loop is untouched.

9
00:00:34.273 --> 00:00:35.353
Its input has dropped.

10
00:00:36.367 --> 00:00:40.047
These are stipulated model values, not a named mountain.

11
00:00:41.527 --> 00:00:44.687
Barometric pressure 500 mmHg.

12
00:00:46.147 --> 00:00:47.967
Subtract water vapour, 47.

13
00:00:49.427 --> 00:00:52.067
21 percent of the rest is about 95.

14
00:00:53.417 --> 00:00:57.997
At sea level this alveolus sat near 100 mmHg.

15
00:00:59.317 --> 00:01:02.517
Here inspired oxygen is already down to 95.

16
00:01:03.997 --> 00:01:05.797
Carbon dioxide is still 40.

17
00:01:07.097 --> 00:01:08.557
So where does the alveolus sit?

18
00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:20.600
Carbon dioxide 40, divided by the exchange ratio 0.80, takes 50 away.

19
00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:27.080
95 minus 50 leaves about 45, less than half the sea level value.

20
00:01:28.133 --> 00:01:34.173
Low oxygen drives harder breathing, and carbon dioxide washes out, here from 40 to

21
00:01:34.173 --> 00:01:40.373
30. 30 divided by 0.80 takes only 37.5 away.

22
00:01:41.693 --> 00:01:43.693
The alveolus rises to about 58.

23
00:01:44.867 --> 00:01:50.547
The gain is twelve and a half millimeters of mercury, ten divided by 0.80.

24
00:01:51.707 --> 00:01:56.827
That is alveolar, not arterial, and a model output rather than a measured benefit.

25
00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:00.490
Down here the oxygen sensor has leverage.

26
00:02:01.790 --> 00:02:07.890
The ventilatory response to alveolar oxygen is shallow above about 60, and steepens

27
00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:10.250
as it enters roughly 50 to 60.

28
00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:14.330
45 and 58 sit in that steep region.

29
00:02:15.383 --> 00:02:16.883
Here is the one idea.

30
00:02:18.343 --> 00:02:20.043
Altitude does not rebuild the loop.

31
00:02:21.283 --> 00:02:25.443
It moves the operating point to where the lever finally buys oxygen.

32
00:02:26.763 --> 00:02:31.183
The hemoglobin curve is steep down here too; what that is worth is asked below.

33
00:02:32.317 --> 00:02:35.217
The climber does not get the full drive at once.

34
00:02:36.357 --> 00:02:41.957
Washing out carbon dioxide raises pH, and that alkalosis inhibits ventilation,

35
00:02:42.617 --> 00:02:44.477
centrally and at the carotid body.

36
00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:51.580
Over days the kidney acts on pH, and the sensors grow more sensitive, so ventilation

37
00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:52.660
can climb further.

38
00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:58.240
These parts move on different clocks, and the text below asks for each one.

39
00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:01.570
So nothing in the controller was rebuilt.

40
00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:07.250
The mountain carried the same loop, and the same single lever, to the part of its

41
00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:08.470
range where a breath counts.
